@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+<|im_start|>user
+Read the following passage carefully, then follow the instructions at the end.
+
+Before the middle of the twentieth century, moving goods across an ocean was a slow, expensive, and labor-intensive affair. Cargo arrived at the docks in an assortment of barrels, sacks, crates, and bales, each of which had to be handled individually. Gangs of longshoremen carried or winched every item out of trucks and railcars, into the hold of a ship, and stowed it piece by piece so the load would not shift at sea. A single freighter could spend a week or more in port, and the cost of loading and unloading often exceeded the cost of the entire ocean crossing. Theft and damage were endemic, insurance was priced accordingly, and any factory that depended on imported parts had to hold months of inventory as a buffer against the unpredictable schedule of break-bulk shipping.
+
+The person most responsible for changing this was not a shipowner but a trucking entrepreneur from North Carolina named Malcom McLean. His insight was that the shipping industry's product was not the voyage but the movement of goods from origin to destination, and that the handling of individual items at every transfer point was the true bottleneck. In 1956 he sent a converted wartime tanker, the Ideal X, from Newark to Houston carrying fifty-eight truck trailer bodies detached from their chassis. The economics were startling: loading the ship cost cents per ton where conventional loading cost dollars per ton. The essential idea of containerization was that a standardized sealed box could be lifted by crane from a truck to a ship to a train without its contents ever being touched.
+
+Standardization was the hard part, and it took a decade of committee warfare. Railroads, truckers, shipowners, and the military all had incompatible preferences for box dimensions and corner fittings. Through the 1960s, negotiations under the International Organization for Standardization eventually settled on the twenty-foot and forty-foot boxes with standardized corner castings that remain the world's units of account for trade today. The corner casting, a small steel block with oblong holes, is arguably the most consequential piece of industrial design of the century: it allowed any crane, chassis, or cell guide anywhere on earth to grip any container, which is what made the box a universal interface rather than one company's equipment.
+
+The consequences arrived in waves. First, the docks themselves transformed. Container cranes and terminals required enormous capital but little labor, so employment for longshoremen collapsed even as tonnage soared, and old urban ports like Manhattan's and central London's gave way to vast greenfield terminals with rail connections and deep water. Second, ship sizes exploded, because once loading time no longer scaled with cargo volume, the economies of scale of a larger hull became decisive. Third, and most profoundly, reliable and cheap ocean transport rewired manufacturing itself. When a box could move from a factory gate in Asia to a warehouse in Europe on a predictable schedule for a trivial cost per item, firms could split production across continents, source components wherever they were cheapest, and run assembly lines on just-in-time deliveries instead of deep inventories. The global supply chain as we know it is, in a real sense, an emergent property of the box.
+
+The system's weaknesses are the mirror image of its strengths. Because the whole network optimizes for the frictionless flow of standardized units, it has little slack, and disruptions propagate globally: a grounded ship in a canal, a closed terminal, or a shortage of empty boxes in the wrong hemisphere can idle factories thousands of miles away. Because the box is sealed and anonymous, customs and security inspection can only ever sample a small fraction of the flow. And because the economics reward scale so heavily, the industry has consolidated into a handful of alliances operating ships so large that only a short list of ports can receive them, concentrating risk at a few choke points.
+
+Instructions: First, summarize the passage above in three detailed paragraphs in your own words. Then list the five most important specific facts the passage contains, as a numbered list. Finally, explain in one paragraph which single claim in the passage you consider most debatable and why.<|im_end|>
